  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">National social protection policies strengthened to extend coverage, improve adequacy of benefits, and enhance sustainability in line with international labour standards</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since 2019 the ILO has supported the Government of China and the social partners in the enhancement of social security through the EU funded Project &#xe2;&#x20ac;&#x153;Improving China&#xe2;&#x20ac;&#x2122;s institutional capacity towards universal social protection&#xe2;&#x20ac;&#x9d; including Phase 1 (July 2019 to December 2022) and Phase 2 (February 2023 to January 2026). The Project brought together expertise and experience from EU and its Member State with the ILO global tools, standards, and technical knowledge to assist China in advancing toward a universal, adequate, and sustainable social security system. Building on the achievements of the first two phases, the ILO, the EU and China are continuing and scaling up their collaboration through Phase 3 (February 2026&#xe2;&#x20ac;&#x201c;January 2030), which will provide further technical support for China on social security reforms in addressing emerging and evolving challenges. In 2026-27 tripartite constituents in China are expected to strengthen capacities to adapt social protection systems to emerging challenges such as population ageing, new forms of employment, climate change and technological advances. ILO technical support will contribute to the formulation and gradual implementation of inclusive, evidence-informed social protection policies aligned with international labour standards, particularly C102 - Social Security (Minimum Standards) Convention. The expected results include: the coverage and benefit adequacy of social protection system enhanced, with a focus on advancing universal social protection; social protection system leveraged for inclusive life and work transitions and structural transformations; policy dialogue and technical exchange strengthened among China, the EU and the ILO to address common social security challenges including climate change, demographic shifts and technological advances.</narrative>
  </description>
